  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nCurrently, the mid/upper level ridge is centered over the Southern \nHigh Plains and Desert Southwest leaving the forecast area on the \nnortheast periphery of the ridge. A shortwave trough is located over \nthe southern Canadian Prairies, digging into the Upper Midwest. At \nthe surface, an associated low pressure is just north of the \nNorthern Plains with a frontal boundary draped southward into \nNebraska. Some remnant showers are centered over central Kansas with \na cloud shield extending over much of the forecast area. For the \nrest of today, a few locations will see light rainfall, especially \nacross central and far south-central Kansas. Temperatures are being \nkept in check for now across the area due to the clouds, but temps \nwill rebound by late afternoon, reaching into the lower 90s for most.\n\nTonight, as the shortwave digs into the Upper Midwest and the \napproaching front begins to push into southern Nebraska, storms are \nexpected to fire along the boundary late this evening. \nSimultaneously, diurnal High Plains thunderstorms will push into \nwestern Kansas. This thunderstorm cluster will gradually push \nsoutheast across Kansas bringing storm chances to central Kansas. \nCurrent guidance suggests the marginal severe threat will be \nconfined west of I-35, with the best chances for severe gusts along \nand west of a line from Salina/Hutch/Pratt. Some showers are \nexpected to linger into the morning across the area, but there's \nstill some uncertainty on the eastward extent. \n\nTemperatures through Friday will remain steady in the upper 80s and \nlower 90s as the shortwave helps to keep the ridge center well \nsouthwest of the Central Plains. Rain chances look minimal through \nthe end of the week behind the frontal boundary. High pressure will \nbuild in and overall drier conditions are expected.\n\nBy this weekend, as the trough over the Great Lakes shifts eastward, \nthe ridge will be allowed to build in over the Southern and Central \nPlains. Heights will rise over the region and temperatures will \nrespond accordingly. Highs for Saturday and Sunday will reach into \nthe upper 90s to lower 100s across the forecast area. A shortwave \nlooks to traverse the Northern Plains on Sunday, bringing a boundary \nand associated rain chances to northern and central Kansas for \nSunday night. The shortwave passage will then shunt the ridge ever \nso slightly south for the tail end of the forecast period. \nTemperatures on Monday and Tuesday are still expected to reach above \nnormal, with highs in the middle 90s for central Kansas and upper \n90s and lower 100s for south-central and southeast Kansas.",
